  "body": "(Edit: The syntax is changed into `blocking=\"render\"` for extensibility. )\r\n\r\n(Please refer to the [full proposal](https://gist.github.com/xiaochengh/9404abbecdc3b45c0e9f3d5e99fbc65d) for formal details and more discussion)\r\n\r\n# Explainer\r\n\r\nAll current browsers already have a render-blocking mechanism: after navigation, the user agent will not render any pixel to the screen before all stylesheets and synchronous scripts in `<head>` are loaded and evaluated (or a UA-defined timeout is reached)<sup>[1](#footnote2)</sup>. This prevents a Flash of Unstyled Contents (FOUC) and ensures critical scripts (like framework code) are executed, so that the page is usable after the first rendering cycle.\r\n\r\nIn this proposal, we extend the above idea and propose a new attribute `blocking` that can be added to `<link>`, `<script>` and `<style>` elements in `<head>`. For now, we would only allow one value `blocking=\"render\"` to support the most demanding use cases, but we would also like to keep the syntax open for [future extensions](https://gist.github.com/xiaochengh/fae2b549b3d37454beeb9028a829f4bd#other-operations-to-blockunblock-on).\r\n  \r\n## Use cases\r\n\r\n- Block rendering on a critical web font to prevent layout shifting or a flash of invisible/unstyled text (see [here](#eliminate-layout-shifting-for-critical-web-fonts) for more details). It also works in the preloading of critical resources of other types, like images, json, etc.\r\n```html\r\n<link blocking=\"render\" rel=\"preload\" href=\"critical-font.woff2\"\r\n      as=\"font\" type=\"font/woff2\" crossorigin>\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Block rendering on script-inserted stylesheets or scripts. This prevents a FOUC when, e.g., the [page](https://bjc.edc.org/bjc-r/course/bjc4nyc.html) uses a [loader script](https://bjc.edc.org/bjc-r/llab/loader.js) to load the actual business stylesheets:\r\n```html\r\n<script>\r\nlet link = document.createElement('link');\r\nlink.rel = 'stylesheet';\r\nlink.href = 'business-style.css';\r\nlink.setAttribute('blocking', 'render');\r\ndocument.head.appendChild(link);\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Block rendering on an async script, so that the script doesn’t block parsing but is guaranteed to be evaluated before rendering starts. This is useful for, e.g., SPA loader and A/B test framework scripts, which can be loaded asynchronously but need control over what is displayed on the first render.\r\n```html\r\n<script blocking=\"render\" async src=\"async-script.js\"></script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Besides, while rendering is blocked, `requestAnimationFrame()` callbacks should not be fired<sup>[2](#footnote3)</sup>. So we can add a `requestAnimationFrame()` in `<head>` to perform some tasks right before the first rendering cycle to, e.g., ensure a better-looking first paint:\r\n```html\r\n<script>\r\nrequestAnimationFrame(() => {\r\n  let status = getResourceLoadingStatus();\r\n  putProgressBarOnPendingResources(status);\r\n});\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Footnotes\r\n\r\n<a name=\"footnote2\">[1]</a> The [exact behaviors](https://github.com/chrishtr/rendering/blob/master/stylesheet-loading-behavior.md) differ slightly.\r\n<a name=\"footnote3\">[2]</a> Current browsers have different behaviors, and this proposal would like to standardize it. See [test case](https://purring-various-felidae.glitch.me/import_test_raf.html).\r\n",
